1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:07,567
Take a box of water at rest, and push it with a smooth force. Follow one drop.

2
00:00:07,567 --> 00:00:11,800
Can you predict whether the drop ever reaches a certain region?

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:21,300
OpenAI claims a force for which the drop gets there exactly when a chosen computer program eventually stops.

4
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:27,400
So water, pushed the right way, can run any program.

5
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:38,867
The Navier–Stokes equations describe how a fluid like water moves:

6
00:00:38,867 --> 00:00:47,967
its momentum, its pressure, its internal friction, and any outside force.

7
00:00:47,967 --> 00:00:59,233
The Millennium problem asks whether smooth flows in three dimensions stay smooth forever, or can break down in finite time.

8
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:07,267
In 2016, Terence Tao suggested a way to attack it: build a fluid that acts like a computer,

9
00:01:07,267 --> 00:01:14,200
programmed to copy itself smaller and faster, again and again.

10
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:23,300
So: does smoothness last? One idea is to make fluids compute.

11
00:01:27,533 --> 00:01:40,167
A Turing machine is the simplest computer: a head on a long tape, reading and writing symbols, following a short table of rules.

12
00:01:40,167 --> 00:01:49,967
Here is a real run. This machine writes four ones, and then stops. We say it halts.

13
00:01:49,967 --> 00:01:58,600
Turing proved that no algorithm can decide, for every machine, whether it will halt.

14
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:08,500
So if a physical system can imitate every machine, some questions about its future cannot be decided either.

15
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:18,100
So machines cannot always be predicted. The question is whether fluids can imitate them.

16
00:02:22,333 --> 00:02:33,967
Here is the claim. Take a fluid in a box with opposite faces glued together, with a fixed viscosity, starting at rest.

17
00:02:33,967 --> 00:02:42,667
For any machine and input, an algorithm writes down a smooth force that stops changing after one unit of time.

18
00:02:42,667 --> 00:02:47,267
It never runs the machine.

19
00:02:47,267 --> 00:02:53,900
With that force, the fluid moves smoothly forever.

20
00:02:53,900 --> 00:03:01,600
And one marked drop enters a fixed strip if and only if the machine halts.

21
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:10,267
So the claim is a force that turns a fluid at rest into any computer.

22
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:17,800
The key idea is Cristopher Moore's.

23
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:24,700
Write the tape left of the head as the digits of one number, and the rest as the digits of another.

24
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:30,567
The whole tape becomes one point in a square.

25
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:42,700
Each step moves the point. Here is the same run as before, computed exactly: it lands in the halting band at step six.

26
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:48,134
Each rule acts on one rectangle by squeezing one way and stretching the other.

27
00:03:48,134 --> 00:03:56,100
Area is kept, which is exactly what a fluid that cannot be compressed does.

28
00:03:56,100 --> 00:04:01,267
But a machine can erase, and a fluid's motion can always be undone.

29
00:04:01,267 --> 00:04:10,134
So the machine first keeps a record of everything it would erase, an idea of Charles Bennett.

30
00:04:10,134 --> 00:04:19,834
Then smooth swirls carry every rectangle to its target, all rules at once, without collisions.

31
00:04:19,834 --> 00:04:31,834
A third direction acts as a clock: drifting up by one unit runs one step. After a gentle start, the flow stops changing.

32
00:04:31,834 --> 00:04:41,567
Finally, the force is read off from the equation: it is whatever makes this chosen flow obey Navier–Stokes.

33
00:04:41,567 --> 00:04:53,134
So: tape becomes point, rules become area-keeping maps, and swirls plus a clock give the force.

34
00:04:57,366 --> 00:05:05,866
OpenAI checked closely related constructions in Lean, including a drop that detects halting in all of space.

35
00:05:05,866 --> 00:05:14,766
This exact theorem is outside that scope, and we found no expert assessment yet.

36
00:05:14,766 --> 00:05:22,500
It does not answer the Millennium problem: these flows stay smooth forever.

37
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:32,566
And it is only one ingredient of Tao's program. Making a fluid compute its own blowup is a much harder step.

38
00:05:32,566 --> 00:05:42,666
But it shows, rigorously, that a pushed fluid can be as unpredictable as any computer.
